  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-1526" textLabel="indole-3-acetonitrile nitrile hydratase / indole-3-acetonitrile nitrilase" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-1526" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="130" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>There are four isoforms of nitrilase in Arabidopsis thaliana. NIT1, NIT2, and NIT3 have similar enzymatic activities based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; experiments |CITS: [11525507][12430025]|, whereas NIT4 has a different pattern of activity |CITS: [11060302]|. NIT3 can convert indole-3-acetonitrile (IAN) to indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) |CITS:[11525507]|. NIT3 can also catalyze the formation of indole-3-acetamide (IAM) from IAN &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; with a ratio of roughly 65% IAA: 35% IAM formation |CITS: [12430025]|. &lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays of NIT3 indicates that IAN is not the preferred substrate of this enzyme. Rather, 3-phenylpropionitrile, most likely a glucosinolate turn-over product in Arabidopsis, is the preferred substrate |CITS: [11525507]|. Thus the physiological role of NIT3 in IAA biosynthesis and its importance in other metabolic processes is not clear |CITS: [18842274]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-1526" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="nitrilase 3" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="nitrilase III" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT3G44320-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33366, RXN-33365, RXN-25278, RXN-16350, RXN-1828" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="5" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation__11060302_" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation__11525507_" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_11060302" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_11525507" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_12430025" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_18842274" />
    </Group>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-1524" textLabel="indole-3-acetonitrile nitrile hydratase / indole-3-acetonitrile nitrilase" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-1524" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="130" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>There are four isoforms of nitrilase in Arabidopsis thaliana. NIT1, NIT2, and NIT3 have similar enzymatic activities based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; experiments |CITS: [11525507][12430025]|, whereas NIT4 has a different pattern of activity |CITS: [11060302]|. NIT2 can convert indole-3-acetonitrile (IAN) to indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) |CITS:[11525507]|. NIT2 can also catalyze the formation of indole-3-acetamide (IAM) from IAN &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; with a ratio of roughly 75% IAA: 25% IAM formation |CITS: [12430025]|. &lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays of NIT2 indicates that IAN is not the preferred substrate of this enzyme. Rather, 3-phenylpropionitrile, most likely a glucosinolate turn-over product in Arabidopsis, is the preferred substrate |CITS: [11525507]|. Thus the physiological role of NIT2 in IAA biosynthesis and its importance in other metabolic processes is not clear |CITS: [18842274]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-1524" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="nitrilase 2" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="nitrilase II" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT3G44300-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33364, RXN-33363, RXN-25277, RXN-16348, RXN-1826" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="5" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
